The reporter's suggested fix is right and safe. In ispconfigconnect_VerifyPermissions' catch block (ispconfigconnect.php:1282), replace the single line with:

    } catch (\Throwable $failure) {
        return ispconfigconnect_isConnectionLevel($failure)
            ? 'Verify Permissions failed: ' . ispconfigconnect_connectionAdvice($failure)
            : ispconfigconnect_errorString('Verify Permissions', $failure);
    }

Why this is safe:
- The 'Verify Permissions failed:' prefix stays. So does the raw message, which connectionAdvice appends in parentheses. The existing unit test in tests/Module/TestConnectionTest.php (~line 245) still passes: it asserts that prefix plus 'mb_scrub', and a PHP \Error is not connection-level, so it stays on errorString.
- MalformedResponseException and SessionExpiredException count as connection-level but have no advice branch; connectionAdvice returns their raw message, so their output is unchanged.
- PermissionDeniedException is not connection-level, so the circular advice "Run Verify Permissions on the service" never appears on this button.
- The one-login latch is not affected.

Tests to add: a unit test per connection-level class (LoginFailed, IpNotAllowed, Lockout, MaintenanceMode, ApiDisabled, Transport) asserting that Verify Permissions returns the same advice sentence as TestConnection, after the 'Verify Permissions failed: ' prefix. Optionally, docs/LIVE-TEST-PLAN.md step 7 could add "press Verify Permissions on a service on ISPC-NEGATIVE; expect the same 'ISPConfig rejected the login ...' advice and one more failed login", but the current plan is not wrong.
